Sealed Secrets was only my suggestion, and we haven't installed or used it.

What you have today

	•	cvs-2-mysql-secret is built by the chart from values.yaml, so the passwords (ChangeMeAppPassword! and the root default) sit in plain text in Git.
	•	The ArgoCD repo and cluster secrets were created through the UI and CLI, not through Git, so they're not in the repo (apart from the cessda-repo-secret.yaml file on your Mac, which you should delete).
	•	The Elasticsearch password is generated by the ECK operator, so it doesn't need Sealed Secrets.

To adopt Sealed Secrets, the work is:

	1.	Install the Sealed Secrets controller on the service cluster, as its own ArgoCD Application.
	2.	Install the kubeseal CLI on your Mac.
	3.	Seal the MySQL credentials:

kubectl create secret generic cvs-2-mysql-secret -n cvs-2 --dry-run=client \
  --from-literal=username=cvs --from-literal=password='<real>' \
  --from-literal=MYSQL_DATABASE=cvs --from-literal=MYSQL_USER=cvs \
  --from-literal=MYSQL_PASSWORD='<real>' --from-literal=MYSQL_ROOT_PASSWORD='<real>' \
  -o yaml | kubeseal --format yaml > cvs/templates/mysql-sealedsecret.yaml

	4.	Delete the plain secret.yaml from the mysql subchart, so only the sealed one exists.
	5.	Commit and let ArgoCD sync.

Two things to watch:

	•	The real password must match what MySQL already stores (the data volume was initialized with it), or the frontend gets Access denied again. Use the current live values, not new ones.
	•	Back up the controller's sealing key, because if the cluster is lost, the sealed files can't be decrypted without it.

